A71 GPU: 24 outstanding jobs instead of 8. All Metal clients (backboardd and the extra slots) share one
NativeAdrenoSubmit with eight job slots. With three clients busy (backboardd, MercuryPosterExtension, and
PosterBoard when the lock screen poster editor opens) Mercury's submit failed with -16 (Resource busy) and
backboardd waited 209 x 500 us for a slot; the owner saw everything restart on a long press of the lock
screen wallpaper. The 32 KiB ring checks its own free space per submission and a full ring rolls the job
back without poisoning, so more slots only raise how much work may be queued.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/unixnu/NativeAdrenoSubmit.h b/iokit/Kernel/arm/unixnu/NativeAdrenoSubmit.h
index 6cc87ea..8af02ad 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/unixnu/NativeAdrenoSubmit.h
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/unixnu/NativeAdrenoSubmit.h
@@ -27,7 +27,7 @@ struct NativeAdrenoSubmitStats {
 /* Kernel-side execbuf: turns validated indirect buffers into one ring
  * submission and owns a device reference per distinct buffer until the
  * timestamp fence is actually observed. This is not a userspace ABI and not a
- * scheduler: at most eight jobs are outstanding, callers serialize every
+ * scheduler: at most 24 jobs are outstanding (2139; was eight), callers serialize every
  * method, and the ring/VM must already be attached to the same live context.
  * Buffers may be sub-ranges of a live VM mapping but never a different IOVA,
  * unmapped handle, table pool or a range that includes unreadable bytes.
@@ -41,7 +41,7 @@ class NativeAdrenoSubmit final {
         NativeAdrenoSubmitResource *resources=nullptr;
         uint32_t queue=0;
         bool active=false;
-    } jobs_[8];
+    } jobs_[24];   // 2139: 8 slots ran out with three Metal clients (Mercury submit -16, backboardd 209 back-offs)
     NativeAdrenoVm *vm_=nullptr;
     NativeAdrenoRing *ring_=nullptr;
     NativeAdrenoSubmitStats stats_{};
